       The CLDR::Number module (the Unicode Common Locale Data Repository)
       knows how to format numbers based on a locale to use its digit grouping
       and separator:

           use CLDR::Number;

           my $number = 1234567890;

           foreach my $locale ( qw(en-US de-DE en-IN) ) {
               my $cldr = CLDR::Number->new( locale => $locale );
               my $decimal = $cldr->decimal_formatter;
               printf "%-5s %s\n", $locale, $decimal->format( $number );
               }

       The output shows the same number formatted according to each locale's
       rules. The bare "en" uses commas between groups of three, the "de" uses
       dots and groups of three, and the "en-IN" uses the 3:2:2 rule with
       commas:

           en-US 1,234,567,890
           de-DE 1.234.567.890
           en-IN 1,23,45,67,890

       CLDR::Number has many more advanced features to handle just about
       anything that you might want. Olaf Alders' article "Stop Writing Your
       Own Commify Functions" has more examples
       ("/www.olafalders.com/2015/09/04/stop-writing-your-own-commify-
       functions/" in https:).

       If you know exactly what you want, a simple subroutine might suffice.
       This example will add separators to your number between groups of three
       digits (or whatever grouping). This uses groups of three and a comma:

           sub commify {
               local $_  = shift;
               1 while s/^([-+]?\d+)(\d{3})/$1,$2/;
               return $_;
           }

       This regex from Benjamin Goldberg does the same thing without the loop:

           s/(^[-+]?\d+?(?=(?>(?:\d{3})+)(?!\d))|\G\d{3}(?=\d))/$1,/g;

       It is easier to see with comments:

           s/(
               ^[-+]?             # beginning of number.
               \d+?               # first digits before first comma
               (?=                # followed by, (but not included in the match) :
                   (?>(?:\d{3})+) # some positive multiple of three digits.
                   (?!\d)         # an *exact* multiple, not x * 3 + 1 or whatever.
               )
               |                  # or:
               \G\d{3}            # after the last group, get three digits
               (?=\d)             # but they have to have more digits after them.
           )/$1,/xg;

